From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sleepy <sleepy@localhost>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 17:32:42 
Subject: [PATCH] mm, swap: apply the xswap guard in do_swapout()

do_swapout() calls __swap_writepage() directly when zswap_store() fails.
The SWP_XSWAP guard that keeps such a folio in memory lives in
swap_writeout(), one level above __swap_writepage(), so this path goes
around it.

That matters because an xswap device is created through
/sys/kernel/mm/xswap/create, not swapon(), so setup_swap_extents() never
runs and sio_pool is never allocated.  __swap_writepage() reaches
swap_add_folio(), which does mempool_alloc(sio_pool, GFP_NOIO); with
sio_pool still NULL that dereferences pool->pool_data at offset 0x18 and
takes the caller down.  Observed on 2026-09-20 as two identical oopses,
kcompressd0 and kswapd0, both from swap_add_folio(), after which the
machine had no kswapd at all.

do_swapout() is reached from two places, do_swapout_batch() (the
kcompressd drain) and kcompressd_store()'s synchronous fallback; the
latter is inlined into swap_writeout(), which is why the kswapd0 oops
reported swap_writeout as its caller rather than do_swapout().

Apply the same guard here.  The folio is unlocked rather than left
locked, because do_swapout() owns the unlock on every branch and
folio_put() follows.

This path is MARIE's: do_swapout() is added by the LRU-MARIE patch, and
the SWP_XSWAP guard by the xswap patch, which applies after it.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Sleepy <sleepy@localhost>
---
 mm/page_io.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

--- a/mm/page_io.c
+++ b/mm/page_io.c
@@ -226,6 +226,17 @@
 	if (zswap_store(folio)) {
 		count_mthp_stat(folio_order(folio), MTHP_STAT_ZSWPOUT);
 		folio_unlock(folio);
+	} else if (unlikely(__swap_entry_to_info(folio->swap)->flags & SWP_XSWAP)) {
+		/*
+		 * xswap has no backing store, so keep the folio.  An xswap
+		 * entry only ever lives in zswap, so reaching here means the
+		 * store was refused.  swap_writeout() carries the same guard,
+		 * but this path calls __swap_writepage() directly and so
+		 * bypasses it - and with it the only place sio_pool would be
+		 * allocated from.
+		 */
+		folio_mark_dirty(folio);
+		folio_unlock(folio);
 	} else
 		__swap_writepage(ctx, folio); /* Implies folio_unlock(folio) */
 
